WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.761 --> 00:00:21.245
my point of view as an expert on crime," said the great detective Sherlock Holmes, "London has become

2
00:00:21.285 --> 00:00:22.346
a rather dull city."

3
00:00:23.226 --> 00:00:26.389
He said this to me as we were finishing our breakfast one morning at Baker Street.

4
00:00:27.329 --> 00:00:30.492
"I doubt you could find many citizens who would agree with you," I replied.

5
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:32.501
"I suppose you are right, Dr.

6
00:00:32.542 --> 00:00:34.964
Watson, and that I must not be so selfish."

7
00:00:35.965 --> 00:00:38.308
Holmes smiled and pushed back his chair from the table.

8
00:00:39.209 --> 00:00:42.433
He was about to unfold his newspaper when we heard our doorbell ringing loudly.

9
00:00:43.274 --> 00:00:47.238
This was immediately followed by a drumming sound as if a man were beating on the outer door

10
00:00:47.278 --> 00:00:47.959
with his fist.

11
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.284
"Perhaps London is about to become a little less dull for you," I remarked.

12
00:00:53.896 --> 00:00:54.777
Our landlady, Mrs.

13
00:00:54.817 --> 00:01:00.280
Hudson, answered the door; someone ran up the stairs; and an instant later a wild-eyed, frantic young man

14
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.240
burst into the room.

15
00:01:02.181 --> 00:01:06.603
Pale and disheveled, he looked from Holmes to me, as if he were trying to determine which one

16
00:01:06.623 --> 00:01:07.924
of us was the great detective.

17
00:01:07.944 --> 00:01:09.645
"I'm sorry, Mr.

18
00:01:09.685 --> 00:01:10.785
Holmes!" he cried.

19
00:01:10.805 --> 00:01:15.008
"You mustn't blame me for interrupting you like this; I'm nearly insane.

20
00:01:15.988 --> 00:01:17.949
I am the unhappy John Hector McFarlane."

21
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.312
He made this announcement as if his name would explain both his visit and his manner.

22
00:01:23.212 --> 00:01:26.815
But I could see by Holmes' face that the name meant no more to him than to me.

23
00:01:26.835 --> 00:01:29.978
"I am sure with your symptoms that my friend Dr.

24
00:01:30.038 --> 00:01:32.720
Watson here could prescribe you a sedative," said Holmes.

25
00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:38.344
"However, I would be glad if you would compose yourself, sit down in that chair, and tell us

26
00:01:38.745 --> 00:01:42.107
very slowly and quietly who you are and what it is that you want.

27
00:01:43.809 --> 00:01:45.770
You mentioned your name as if I should recognize it.

28
00:01:46.631 --> 00:01:50.294
But I assure you that beyond the obvious facts that you are a bachelor, a lawyer, and an

29
00:01:50.434 --> 00:01:52.336
asthmatic, I know nothing about you."

30
00:01:53.236 --> 00:01:57.840
I was familiar with Holmes' methods, so it wasn't difficult for me to follow his deductions after I

31
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:04.025
observed the man's untidy attire, the sheaf of legal papers protruding from his overcoat pocket, and his labored

32
00:02:04.045 --> 00:02:07.788
breathing. Our client, however, stared at Holmes in amazement.

33
00:02:07.808 --> 00:02:10.190
"Yes, I am all of that, Mr.

34
00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:14.113
Holmes, and in addition I am the most unfortunate man in London," he said.

35
00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:16.555
"For heaven's sake, don't abandon me!

36
00:02:17.496 --> 00:02:21.539
If the police come to arrest me before I have finished my story, make them give me time

37
00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:22.580
to tell you the whole truth.

38
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.101
I could go to jail happy if I knew that you were working for me on the outside."

39
00:02:27.121 --> 00:02:29.522
"Arrest you!"

40
00:02:29.902 --> 00:02:30.462
said Holmes.

41
00:02:30.482 --> 00:02:32.963
"This is really most gratifying.

42
00:02:33.943 --> 00:02:36.304
I mean, most interesting," he corrected himself.

43
00:02:36.324 --> 00:02:39.105
"On what charge do you expect to be arrested?"

44
00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:41.445
"Upon the charge of murdering Mr.

45
00:02:41.505 --> 00:02:43.106
Jonas Oldacre of Lower Norwood."

46
00:02:43.126 --> 00:02:45.586
"Dear me," said Holmes.

47
00:02:45.606 --> 00:02:47.707
"I was only just saying to Dr.

48
00:02:47.747 --> 00:02:50.728
Watson that interesting criminal cases had disappeared from London."

49
00:02:51.708 --> 00:02:56.549
Our visitor stretched forth a quivering hand and picked up the newspaper that still lay upon Holmes' knee.

50
00:02:56.569 --> 00:03:01.651
"If you had only glanced at this morning's newspaper, sir, you would have seen why I am here,"

51
00:03:02.011 --> 00:03:02.691
said McFarlane.

52
00:03:03.571 --> 00:03:05.652
"I feel as if my name must be on everyone's lips."

53
00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:11.014
He opened the newspaper and showed us the headlines: "Mysterious Affair at Lower Norwood.

54
00:03:12.554 --> 00:03:14.455
Disappearance of a Well-known Builder.

55
00:03:15.455 --> 00:03:17.135
Suspicion of Murder and Arson.

56
00:03:18.096 --> 00:03:19.076
A Clue to the Criminal."

57
00:03:19.948 --> 00:03:23.511
"That clue mentioned in the headline leads directly to me," said McFarlane.

58
00:03:23.531 --> 00:03:27.054
"I am sure the police are only waiting for the warrant to arrest me."

59
00:03:28.015 --> 00:03:31.958
As he wrung his hands in agony and swayed back and forth in his chair, I looked with

60
00:03:32.018 --> 00:03:34.140
interest upon this young man accused of murder.

61
00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.202
He was blond and handsome with frightened blue eyes.

62
00:03:38.163 --> 00:03:42.927
He seemed to be about 27 years old, and his dress and bearing were those of a gentleman.

63
00:03:42.947 --> 00:03:46.310
"We must use the time we have," said Holmes.

64
00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:49.092
"Watson, please read this article aloud."

65
00:03:49.928 --> 00:03:55.570
I picked up the newspaper and read the following: Late last night an incident occurred in Norwood that

66
00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.390
seems to point to a serious crime.

67
00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:02.731
Mr. Jonas Oldacre, age 52, is a well-known builder who resides in that suburb.

68
00:04:03.672 --> 00:04:07.033
A bachelor, he has the reputation for being eccentric and secretive.

69
00:04:07.953 --> 00:04:12.394
For some years he has practically withdrawn from his business, in which he is said to have made

70
00:04:12.434 --> 00:04:13.614
a considerable fortune.

71
00:04:14.595 --> 00:04:19.356
A small lumberyard still exists behind his house, and around midnight a fire was reported there.

72
00:04:20.352 --> 00:04:24.353
It was a serious fire, consuming one of the stacks of lumber, so the firemen were surprised that

73
00:04:24.393 --> 00:04:26.394
Mr. Oldacre did not emerge from the house.

74
00:04:27.474 --> 00:04:31.775
An investigation later showed that he had disappeared from the house and his bed had not been slept

75
00:04:31.835 --> 00:04:36.557
in. The safe in his bedroom was open, and a number of important papers were scattered about the

76
00:04:36.597 --> 00:04:40.338
room. There were also signs of a murderous struggle.

77
00:04:41.238 --> 00:04:44.979
Blood was found in the bedroom and also upon the handle of an oak walking stick.

78
00:04:45.939 --> 00:04:50.993
Mr. Oldacre had received a visitor late last night, and the walking stick has been identified as belonging

79
00:04:51.013 --> 00:04:54.174
to his visitor, a London lawyer named John Henry McFarlane.

80
00:04:55.074 --> 00:04:59.435
The story continued with an update: It is now reported that the French windows of Mr.

81
00:04:59.515 --> 00:05:02.216
Oldacre's bedroom, which is on the ground floor, were open.

82
00:05:03.176 --> 00:05:07.937
It appeared that a bulky object had been dragged out to the lumberyard, and charred remains were found

83
00:05:08.037 --> 00:05:09.358
among the ashes of the fire.

84
00:05:10.298 --> 00:05:15.039
The police theory is that the victim was clubbed to death in his bedroom, his papers rifled, and

85
00:05:15.079 --> 00:05:19.213
his body dragged out to the lumberyard, where a fire was ignited to hide the crime.

86
00:05:20.254 --> 00:05:23.075
Inspector Lestrade of Scotland Yard is leading the investigation.

87
00:05:24.756 --> 00:05:29.399
Sherlock Holmes listened to this remarkable story with his eyes closed and his fingertips pressed together.

88
00:05:29.419 --> 00:05:33.301
"The case certainly has some points of interest," he remarked.

89
00:05:33.321 --> 00:05:35.102
"May I ask, Mr.

90
00:05:35.122 --> 00:05:39.985
McFarlane, why you are still free since there seems to be enough evidence to justify your arrest?"

91
00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:44.948
"I live at Torrington Lodge, Blackheath, with my parents," replied the young man.

92
00:05:45.972 --> 00:05:48.093
"Last night by the time I finished my business with Mr.

93
00:05:48.173 --> 00:05:51.915
Oldacre, it was too late to go home, so I stayed at a hotel in Norwood.

94
00:05:52.875 --> 00:05:56.236
I knew nothing of this affair until I was on the train and read the story in the

95
00:05:56.276 --> 00:06:01.619
newspaper." He explained that he had immediately realized the danger he was in and had hurried to put

96
00:06:01.659 --> 00:06:03.460
his case in the hands of Sherlock Holmes.

97
00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:08.442
"I have no doubt that I would have been arrested at my office or my home," he said.

98
00:06:08.462 --> 00:06:12.583
"A man followed me from London Bridge Station, and—good heavens!

99
00:06:13.064 --> 00:06:13.544
What's that?"

100
00:06:14.516 --> 00:06:18.637
It was the ringing of our doorbell that had startled our visitor, followed instantly by the sound of

101
00:06:18.717 --> 00:06:20.038
heavy steps upon the stairs.

102
00:06:21.038 --> 00:06:24.279
A moment later our old friend Inspector Lestrade appeared in the doorway.

103
00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:28.700
Over his shoulder I caught a glimpse of two uniformed policemen in the hallway.

104
00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.081
"Mr. John Hector McFarlane?"

105
00:06:31.421 --> 00:06:32.081
said Lestrade.

106
00:06:32.961 --> 00:06:35.482
Our client stood up with a desperate expression on his face.

107
00:06:35.502 --> 00:06:40.503
"You are under arrest," said Lestrade, "for the willful murder of Mr.

108
00:06:40.583 --> 00:06:42.524
Jonas Oldacre of Lower Norwood."
